1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:10,985
A processor has only thirty-two registers, but a real program has arrays,

2
00:00:10,985 --> 00:00:14,459
structures and thousands upon thousands of variables.

3
00:00:14,459 --> 00:00:18,766
Whatever doesn't fit in the registers has to live in memory.

4
00:00:18,766 --> 00:00:25,939
RISC-V is what we call a load-store architecture, which means arithmetic instructions work only on registers,

5
00:00:25,939 --> 00:00:29,032
and reaching memory takes special instructions.

6
00:00:29,032 --> 00:00:37,100
A load copies data from memory into a register, and a store writes a register's value back out to memory.

7
00:00:37,100 --> 00:00:45,198
You can picture memory as one enormous array of bytes, where every byte has its own number, called its address.

8
00:00:45,198 --> 00:00:52,422
To keep the picture readable we draw four bytes per row, so the first row runs from 0x100 to 0x103,

9
00:00:52,422 --> 00:00:55,200
and the next row starts at 0x104.

10
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:01,008
A word takes four bytes, so the addresses of neighboring words differ by four, not by one.

11
00:01:01,008 --> 00:01:05,718
Word addresses are normally multiples of four, which is called alignment,

12
00:01:05,718 --> 00:01:10,966
and a misaligned access can be slower or even fail outright.

13
00:01:10,966 --> 00:01:16,354
How do we fit a thirty-two bit number, such as 0x12345678, into four bytes?

14
00:01:16,354 --> 00:01:24,566
RISC-V uses what's called little-endian order, which puts the least significant byte at the smallest address.

15
00:01:24,566 --> 00:01:30,298
So reading upward from 0x100, the bytes are seven eight, five six, three four and one two,

16
00:01:30,298 --> 00:01:33,419
which looks like the number was stored backwards.

17
00:01:33,419 --> 00:01:38,833
The good news is that loading or storing a whole word reassembles the number for you,

18
00:01:38,833 --> 00:01:43,066
so you rarely need to think about byte order.

19
00:01:43,066 --> 00:01:50,488
Now suppose memory holds the integer array A, which starts at address 0x100 and uses one word per element.

20
00:01:50,488 --> 00:01:57,866
The address where the array begins is called the base address, and we'll keep it in the register s0.

21
00:01:57,866 --> 00:02:01,423
The instruction lw stands for load word.

22
00:02:01,423 --> 00:02:09,160
Take lw t0, 8(s0), which reads one word from the address s0 plus 8 and puts it into t0.

23
00:02:09,160 --> 00:02:18,266
The offset, 8, plus the base address gives 0x108, and that is exactly where A[2] lives.

24
00:02:18,266 --> 00:02:22,976
So in C, the element A[i] sits at a byte offset of four times i.

25
00:02:22,976 --> 00:02:32,666
For example, A[3] is at offset 12, not 3, and mixing those two up is one of the most common mistakes in assembly.

26
00:02:32,666 --> 00:02:38,609
The store instruction sw goes the other way and writes a register's value into memory.

27
00:02:38,609 --> 00:02:45,727
Let's work through a complete example, where element three of the array A is set to h plus element one,

28
00:02:45,727 --> 00:02:47,110
and h is kept in s1.

29
00:02:47,110 --> 00:02:50,866
First we load element one into a register.

30
00:02:50,866 --> 00:02:55,553
Next we add h, so t0 becomes ten plus twelve, which is twenty-two.

31
00:02:55,553 --> 00:02:59,175
Finally sw writes that value back to element three,

32
00:02:59,175 --> 00:03:05,300
and notice that sw lists the source register first and the memory address second.

33
00:03:05,300 --> 00:03:13,723
In hexadecimal, twenty-two is 0x16, so with little-endian order the lowest address, 0x10C, holds the byte 0x16,

34
00:03:13,723 --> 00:03:17,833
and the three bytes after it hold zero.

35
00:03:17,833 --> 00:03:24,963
Besides whole words, RISC-V can also read and write a single byte, using the instructions lb, lbu, and sb.

36
00:03:24,963 --> 00:03:31,017
When lb loads a byte into a thirty-two bit register, twenty-four extra bits are left over,

37
00:03:31,017 --> 00:03:33,733
so what should they be filled with?

38
00:03:33,733 --> 00:03:40,315
The answer is sign extension, which copies the top bit, the sign bit, into all the higher bits.

39
00:03:40,315 --> 00:03:46,619
The top bit of 0xF3 is a one, so all twenty-four upper bits become ones, giving 0xFFFFFFF3.

40
00:03:46,619 --> 00:03:53,300
As a signed number, 0xF3 is minus thirteen, and after extension it is still minus thirteen.

41
00:03:53,300 --> 00:03:59,569
The unsigned version, lbu, always fills the upper bits with zeros, so the result is 243.

42
00:03:59,569 --> 00:04:01,421
And sb goes the other way,

43
00:04:01,421 --> 00:04:09,300
writing only the lowest eight bits of a register to memory and ignoring the other twenty-four.

44
00:04:09,300 --> 00:04:15,864
Halfwords, which are sixteen bits, follow the same rules with lh, lhu, and sh.

45
00:04:15,864 --> 00:04:25,400
And since lw already reads a full thirty-two bits, it needs no extension, which is why RV32 has no lwu.

